
  [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m stays in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. It's pure and would be more testable in [38;5;153msrc/[39m, but it has nothing to do with tracking and
  moving it is scope creep.

  [1mOne conflict I have to flag.[0m [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153m"type"[39m field, so Node treats every [38;5;153m.js[39m as CommonJS — which means
  [38;5;153msrc/auth.js[39m written as ESM will fail to load under the test runner even though the browser loads it fine. The fix is
  [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, which then breaks [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m (both CommonJS). Those are 7 and 5
  lines respectively, so converting them is a two-minute mechanical edit. I'd rather do that than name the new files
  [38;5;153m.mjs[39m and leave a permanent oddity. It does mean this change touches two files that have nothing to do with login —
  required for correctness, not cleanup.

  [1mThe scope question I can't answer for you:[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m today is a stub that unconditionally returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user:
[39m  [38;5;153musername }[39m and never contacts [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. That matters because your chosen design has a failure event and a real
  [38;5;153muserId[39m — and a stub produces neither. Either [38;5;153mlogin()[39m starts actually calling the endpoint, or the failure path is
  unreachable code and [38;5;153muserId[39m is a fabricated value.

